  "notes": "Blues-and-gospel-rooted soul-rock, straight 2&4 \"with heavy stomp-and-clap substitution\". The drums are authored from that substitution: \"floor-tom and stomp patterns with handclaps on the backbeat, and a loose played kit that enters late; gospel-style tambourine on offbeat 8ths.\" So the snare stack is `clap` alone with a wide human offset, the perc lanes are `tomLow`, `tom` and `tambourine` with the tambourine driving the `offbeat` placement, the hat runs 8ths at a low fill density with no roll vocabulary, and the humanize block is the loose played kind. No `bass808` block: \"no 808. Electric or upright bass on roots with a walking blues motion; the low end is often just the guitar's bass strings in verse one\", so the bassline is `independent_riff` at 0.44 with a full walking passing-tone set. Harmony is \"2- or 4-bar minor vamps\" over \"modal (Dorian/Aeolian) vamps\" with \"blues-scale melodic borrowing\", so `scales` carries dorian and `blues` at real weight and the families are short and vamp-led. The melody block encodes \"blues-inflected with bent thirds and long melismatic descents\" as `colorTones` on the flat third and seventh, the highest `descend` weight in this batch and `endVariation` for the melisma, over the widest register span in this batch because the entry documents \"a very wide range, written low and gravelly in verses and pushed into a high, rasped belt for choruses\". \"Massed gospel-choir backing is the signature countermelody\" is `sustain_pad` at top weight. ⚠ `confidence` is medium and the research says the parameters are \"lane-conventional\": every drum probability, the humanize numbers, the harmonic weights and `bpm.mode` 100 (the midpoint of the stated 80—120 core) are INFERRED, and the entry's [LOW-CONF] mark on the baritone-to-tenor range claim is repeated here.",
